{
 "id": "2022-ferreira-tagus",
 "name": "Tagus Nature Reserve Landscapes: Pairwise Preference (AHP)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Ferreira & Sánchez-Martín (2022), \"The Assessment of the Tourism Potential of the Tagus International Nature Reserve Landscapes\", Land 11:68. Participants compare six photographs of landscape types in the Tagus International Nature Reserve (Spain–Portugal border) in all 15 pairs, on the paper's 1–9 scale (1 = absolute preference for the left picture, 5 = equal, 9 = absolute preference for the right picture), for outdoor / recreational activities. They then choose their preferred landscape type without photographs and give gender, age, study level, place of residence and job (Table S1). Landscapes: dehesa/montado, dehesa/montado with stockbreeding, traditional olive grove, Mediterranean forest and scrubland, rural settlements, rivers and water bodies. Analysis: AHP (Saaty) on the pairwise matrix, principal-eigenvector weights and consistency ratio; chi-square tests of the no-photo choice against socio-demographic groups. In the original study (174 local residents and visitors, 2020), the AHP weights ranked rural settlements first (20.3%) and rivers and water bodies last (11.8%).\n\nPhotographs: the paper's Figure 4 (taken by the authors in 2019; article CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the 15 pairs are shown in the order of Table S1 with the left/right position as listed there; the paper does not say whether order or sides were varied; (2) the wording of the pairwise and no-photo questions is our own, built from Table S1 and the method text (the original questionnaire wording, in Portuguese/Spanish, is not published); (3) the other questions on knowledge of and experience in the territory and the \"landscape that conveys positive feelings\" item mentioned in the paper are not included because their wording is not given; (4) job is a free-text field, as the paper lists it only as \"multiple responses\" without options; (5) an online consent checkbox was added.",
